</style>
  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Three councils, one set of state rules</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Your building permit comes from the City of Cockburn, the City of Melville or the City of Canning, depending on where you live. The rules underneath are the same across WA: the Building Regulations 2012 say that removing a load-bearing wall, changing floor area or height, or replacing footings is not exempt work, so it needs a permit.</p><p>You can apply two ways. A certified application (BA1) comes with a certificate of design compliance from a private building surveyor, and the council has 10 business days to decide. An uncertified application (BA2) goes to the council's own surveyors and has 25 business days. Either way the clock stops if the council asks for more information, so engineering that answers the surveyor's questions first time can save you weeks.</p><p>Permit fees are set by regulation: 0.19% of the estimated value of the work for a certified permit, or 0.32% for an uncertified one, with a minimum of $121.00. The value is counted including GST. Our <a href="/structural-engineer-cost-perth/">cost guide</a> has the state levies that sit on top.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Cracks in sand country</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Sand is the most common soil in metropolitan Perth, and a sand site is normally Class A under AS 2870, with little movement between wet and dry seasons. Geoscience Australia maps Bassendean Sand across much of central Perth, with Spearwood sand and Tamala Limestone along the coastal strip. Nobody has published which soil sits under which southern suburb, so the engineer judges your block when they see it.</p><p>Sand doesn't shrink and swell like clay, but CSIRO says it is particularly easy to wash away. A leaking pipe, a downpipe that dumps roof water beside the footings, or reticulation left on too long can all undermine a footing. In brickwork that tends to show as step cracks in the mortar or vertical cracks at doors and windows.</p><p>CSIRO grades cracks by width, from under 0.1 mm (negligible) up to 15 to 25 mm (severe). A tape measure tells you which band a crack is in, but not what caused it or whether it's still moving. That's what a <a href="/structural-inspections/">structural inspection</a> is for.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Check whether you need an engineer first</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Some small jobs are exempt from a building permit. A pergola is exempt if it has no roof, is no more than 2.4 m high, covers 20 m2 or less and isn't in wind region C or D. A freestanding shed can be exempt up to 10 m2 and 2.4 m high, on the same wind condition. A roofed patio doesn't count as a pergola, so it usually needs a permit and, with it, engineering.</p><p>If your job falls outside those limits, have photos, any existing plans and a rough sketch ready when you call. See <a href="/patio-shed-certificates/">patio and shed certificates</a>, <a href="/wall-removal-engineering/">wall removal engineering</a> or <a href="/extension-engineering/">extension engineering</a> for what each job involves.</p><p>From 1 July 2027, structural engineering work in WA must be done by a registered building engineer. You can look up any engineer on the DEMIRS Building Engineer Register before you sign anything, and it's worth the two minutes.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F1ECE2;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">I built a patio in Canning without a permit. What now?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Unauthorised work is regularised with a building approval certificate, and the City of Canning uses form BA13 for unauthorised Class 1 or Class 10 work. The state fee is 0.38% of the estimated value, minimum $121.00. The process Perth firms describe includes a building surveyor's certificate and an engineer's inspection and certification of what was built, which the engineer quotes after seeing photos or the site.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How long does a building permit take in Cockburn, Melville or Canning?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The regulations give the council 10 business days for a certified application and 25 business days for an uncertified one, unless you agree in writing to longer. The clock stops if the council asks for more information. Engineering has to be done before you lodge, so ask the engineer for their turnaround when they quote.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">What does a structural engineer cost in the southern suburbs?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Perth engineering firms generally don't publish prices, and we won't guess one for you. Engineers quote each job, and the price depends on the house, the structure and what the council needs. Government charges are fixed, though: a certified permit is 0.19% of the value of the work with a $121.00 minimum. Our <a href="/structural-engineer-cost-perth/">cost page</a> has the rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Do you cover the whole of Cockburn, Melville and Canning?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Yes. Enquiries from anywhere in the three council areas go to an independent engineering firm, which quotes you directly. If you're nearer Rockingham or the coast, see our <a href="/structural-engineer-rockingham-kwinana/">Rockingham and Kwinana</a> or <a href="/structural-engineer-western-suburbs-fremantle/">Western Suburbs and Fremantle</a> pages. Otherwise call (08) 5550 6237.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
